# Every pattern here is anchored to the repository root with a leading slash, and that
# is a correctness requirement rather than a style. A gitignore pattern containing no
# slash of its own matches at *every* level of the tree, which is how `config/` in
# labview/.gitignore came to swallow labview/internal/config — an ignored file is not an
# untracked one, so `git status` stayed clean while CI checked out a module missing a
# package it imports.
#
# `.env` was the same trap, one new file away from firing: §6.4's `env_file` behaviour is
# tested by fixtures that are literally named `.env`, and the ten under labview/fixtures
# survive only because they were committed before this rule existed. An eleventh would
# have been ignored without a word.

# Sample lab configuration used as scan input, and a scratch checkout beside it. Neither
# is part of this repository. No trailing slash, so a stray file by either name is caught
# as well as a directory.
/labview-bob
/truenas-apps

# Compose reads .env from the directory holding the compose file, so there are exactly two
# places one can legitimately appear: beside this file, and beside labview/compose.yml.
/.env
/labview/.env
